     "difficulty": "hard_hots",
     "question": "At what Celsius temperature is the numerical value on the Fahrenheit scale exactly twice that of the Celsius scale ($F = 2C$)?",
     "options": [
      "(a) 160°C",
      "(b) 320°C",
      "(c) 80°C",
      "(d) 100°C"
     ],
     "answer": "(a) 160°C",
     "explanation": "Using $F = \\frac{9}{5}C + 32$ and substituting $F = 2C$: $2C = \\frac{9}{5}C + 32 \\implies 2C - 1.8C = 32 \\implies 0.2C = 32 \\implies C = \\frac{32}{0.2} = 160^\\circ\\text{C}$ (Fahrenheit value is $320^\\circ\\text{F}$)."
