     "type": "short_answer",
     "marks": 3,
     "difficulty": "medium",
     "question": "Explain why an ice box is constructed with double walls and the space between them is packed with cork or thermocol.",
     "options": [],
     "answer": "Ice melts when it absorbs heat from the surrounding environment. An ice box utilizes a double-wall design packed with insulating materials (cork, glass wool, or thermocol) containing trapped air pockets. Because air and cork are poor thermal conductors, this barrier drastically retards the flow of external heat into the box, preserving the ice for extended periods.",
