     "question": "On a cold morning, a metal chair and a wooden chair are in the same room. Why does the metal chair feel much colder when touched?",
     "options": [],
     "answer": "Both chairs are in thermal equilibrium at the exact same room temperature. However, metal has high thermal conductivity and draws heat rapidly away from your warm finger ($37^\\circ\\text{C}$). Wood has very low thermal conductivity and conducts heat away very slowly; hence your skin senses metal as colder.",
