    {
     "id": "phy_ch2_sa_015",
     "subject": "science",
     "chapter_num": "2",
     "chapter_title": "Heat Transfer & Temperature",
     "type": "long_answer",
     "marks": 5,
     "difficulty": "hard_hots",
     "question": "Explain the complete construction and working of a Thermos flask (vacuum flask), showing how all three modes of heat transfer are minimized.",
     "options": [],
     "answer": "A Thermos flask maintains hot liquids hot and cold liquids cold by establishing barriers against all three modes of heat transfer:Conduction & Convection Blocked: The flask consists of a double-walled glass or stainless steel vessel. The air in the space between the double walls is completely evacuated to create a high vacuum. Since conduction and convection require matter, neither mode can cross this vacuum barrier.Radiation Blocked: The glass walls facing the vacuum are coated with reflective silvering (mirrors). The inner shiny wall reflects infrared radiation from the hot liquid back inside, while the outer silvered wall reflects external ambient radiation away.Neck & Stopper Insulation: The mouth is sealed with a tight-fitting stopper made of cork, silicone, or hollow plastic (poor conductors), eliminating conductive heat loss through the neck and preventing evaporation.",
     "explanation": "Official textbook model solution and marking rubric breakdown."
    },
    {
     "id": "phy_ch2_sa_016",
     "subject": "science",
     "chapter_num": "2",
     "chapter_title": "Heat Transfer & Temperature",
     "type": "short_answer",
     "marks": 3,
     "difficulty": "medium",
     "question": "What is anomalous expansion of water? Explain how this unique phenomenon preserves aquatic life during freezing winter.",
     "options": [],
     "answer": "Most liquids contract continuously as they cool. Water behaves abnormally: between $4^\\circ\\text{C}$ and $0^\\circ\\text{C}$, water actually expands as it cools, reaching its maximum density at exactly $4^\\circ\\text{C}$.Aquatic Preservation: In winter, as surface water cools to $4^\\circ\\text{C}$, it becomes densest and sinks to the bottom of the lake. When the surface further cools below $4^\\circ\\text{C}$, it becomes lighter and remains at the top, eventually freezing into an ice crust at $0^\\circ\\text{C}$. Since ice is a poor conductor of heat, it insulates the water underneath, maintaining a liquid bottom layer at $4^\\circ\\text{C}$ where fish and plants survive comfortably.",
     "explanation": "Official textbook model solution and marking rubric breakdown."
    },
    {
     "id": "phy_ch2_sa_017",
     "subject": "science",
     "chapter_num": "2",
     "chapter_title": "Heat Transfer & Temperature",
     "type": "short_answer",
     "marks": 3,
     "difficulty": "medium",
     "question": "Why can a thin paper cup containing water be held directly over a burner flame without catching fire?",
     "options": [],
     "answer": "Paper has an ignition temperature of approximately $233^\\circ\\text{C}$. When water is in the paper cup, the heat supplied by the flame is rapidly conducted through the thin paper to the water due to water's immense heat capacity. The temperature of the paper never exceeds the boiling point of water ($100^\\circ\\text{C}$); because this is far below the paper's ignition point, the paper cannot catch fire as long as water remains.",
     "explanation": "Official textbook model solution and marking rubric breakdown."
    }
