    {
     "id": "civ17_sa_ext_001",
     "subject": "science",
     "chapter_num": "17",
     "chapter_title": "Markets Around Us",
     "type": "short_answer",
     "marks": 3,
     "difficulty": "medium",
     "question": "What is meant by a 'Chain of Markets'? Why is it necessary?",
     "options": [],
     "answer": "• **Definition**: A chain of markets refers to the series of interconnected commercial links through which goods travel from original producers (farmers, factory owners) to wholesale traders, sub-dealers, local retailers, and ultimately to consumers. • **Necessity**: Producers rarely sell small quantities directly to individual consumers. Wholesale markets aggregate goods in bulk, transport them across regional boundaries, and distribute them to local neighborhood shops.",
     "explanation": "Fundamental economic distribution principle in Civics."
    },
    {
     "id": "civ17_la_ext_001",
     "subject": "science",
     "chapter_num": "17",
     "chapter_title": "Markets Around Us",
     "type": "long_answer",
     "marks": 5,
     "difficulty": "hard_hots",
     "question": "Compare weekly market traders with shop owners in urban shopping complexes. How does this demonstrate economic inequality?",
     "options": [],
     "answer": "• **Capital Investment**: Weekly market vendors invest very small sums of money (often assisted by family members with no rent or permanent electricity bills). Mall shop owners invest huge capital in air-conditioned premises, branding, advertising, and staff salaries. • **Pricing & Profits**: Weekly market sellers sell unbranded goods at cheap, negotiable rates with modest profit margins. Mall outlets sell expensive branded luxury products with high markups. • **Customer Base**: Weekly markets serve ordinary workers, daily-wage laborers, and rural households looking for budget items. Shopping malls cater primarily to affluent urban upper-middle-class consumers. • **Inequality**: Both participate in markets, but their financial gains, risk resilience, and social leverage are vastly unequal.",
     "explanation": "Critical comparative analysis required by CBSE curriculum."
    }
