     "difficulty": "hard_hots",
     "question": "How did the widespread adoption of iron technology and new irrigation techniques transform agriculture and society during this period?",
     "options": [],
     "answer": "• **Iron Ploughshares**: Replaced wooden ploughs, allowing heavy, clayey soil of the fertile Gangetic plain to be tilled deeply, drastically multiplying crop yields. • **Iron Axes**: Facilitated systematic clearance of dense subtropical forests for expanding agricultural settlements. • **Paddy Transplantation**: Instead of broadcasting seeds, farmers raised saplings in seedbeds and transplanted them into waterlogged fields, ensuring higher seedling survival rates. • **State-Supported Irrigation**: Kings and villages constructed extensive networks of canals, wells, artificial irrigation tanks, and reservoirs, raising agricultural surpluses that supported flourishing urban craft centers.",
