     "difficulty": "hard_hots",
     "question": "There are 15 coins placed on a table, all showing heads. In each turn, you must flip exactly 2 coins simultaneously. Can you ever reach a state where all 15 coins show tails? Justify mathematically using parity invariance.",
     "options": [],
     "answer": "• **Mathematical Formulation**: - Let the number of tails be $T$. - Initially, all coins are heads, so $T = 0$ (an **even** number). - Goal: Reach all 15 coins showing tails, meaning $T = 15$ (an **odd** number). • **Analyzing the Flipping Operation**: When you flip exactly 2 coins, three cases arise: 1. Both were heads $\\rightarrow$ both become tails: $T$ increases by 2 ($T + 2$). 2. Both were tails $\\rightarrow$ both become heads: $T$ decreases by 2 ($T - 2$). 3. One was head and one was tail $\\rightarrow$ they swap: $T$ remains unchanged ($T + 0$). • **Parity Invariance**: In all three cases, the change in $T$ is $+2, -2,$ or $0$. Therefore, **the parity of $T$ (evenness or oddness) never changes**; it remains strictly invariant. • **Conclusion**: Since $T$ starts as an even number ($0$), it can **never** become an odd number ($15$). Hence, it is impossible to reach all tails.",
