     "options": [],
     "answer": "Draw three triangles in which the altitudes lie inside, on the side, and outside the triangles. View Solution & Descriptions 1. Inside the triangle: In an Acute-Angled Triangle (e.g. all angles $< 90^\\circ$), all three altitudes lie entirely inside the triangle, intersecting at an interior orthocenter. 2. On the side of the triangle: In a Right-Angled Triangle , the two perpendicular legs forming the right angle are themselves the altitudes! They lie on the sides , meeting at the right-angle vertex. 3. Outside the triangle: In an Obtuse-Angled Triangle (having one angle $> 90^\\circ$), the altitudes drawn from the two acute vertices drop onto the extended base lines, thus lying strictly outside the triangle.",
