    {
     "id": "eng_gram1_la_001",
     "subject": "english",
     "chapter_num": "Gram_Ch01",
     "chapter_title": "Chapter 1: Nouns — Formation & Classification",
     "type": "long_answer",
     "marks": 5,
     "difficulty": "hard",
     "question": "Explain the three structural classifications of Compound Nouns in English with two clear examples for each category. Discuss how compound nouns differ from noun phrases.",
     "options": [],
     "answer": "1. Three Structural Classifications of Compound Nouns: • Open (Spaced) Compound Nouns: Written as distinct individual words with spaces separating them, yet functioning syntactically and semantically as a single lexical unit (e.g., 'ice cream', 'swimming pool', 'post office'). • Hyphenated Compound Nouns: Connected by hyphens to bind multi-word compounds, common in titles, relations, and prepositional phrases (e.g., 'mother-in-law', 'passer-by', 'editor-in-chief'). • Closed (Solid) Compound Nouns: Fused into a single orthographic word without hyphens or spaces, often originating as older compounds that became standardized over time (e.g., 'toothpaste', 'keyboard', 'sunflower'). 2. Distinction from Noun Phrases: A compound noun acts as a unified semantic whole where the first element modifies or specifies the second element to create a distinct item with an idiomatic meaning, whereas a standard noun phrase merely combines a descriptive adjective with a noun (e.g., 'a black board' is any wooden plank painted black, but 'a blackboard' is an educational writing surface).",
     "explanation": "Evaluated on precise categorization of open, hyphenated, and closed forms, accompanied by accurate examples and distinction from descriptive noun phrases."
    },
    {
     "id": "eng_gram1_la_002",
     "subject": "english",
     "chapter_num": "Gram_Ch01",
     "chapter_title": "Chapter 1: Nouns — Formation & Classification",
     "type": "long_answer",
     "marks": 5,
     "difficulty": "hard",
     "question": "Describe how Abstract Nouns and Agent Nouns are formed from verbs and adjectives using derivational suffixes. Provide five distinct suffixes with corresponding base words and derived nouns.",
     "options": [],
     "answer": "Noun formation in English frequently utilizes derivational suffixes appended to adjectives and verbal stems: 1. Suffix '-ness' (from adjectives): Creates abstract nouns denoting state or condition (e.g., kind -> kindness, dark -> darkness). 2. Suffix '-tion' / '-sion' (from verbs): Creates abstract nouns denoting action or outcome (e.g., educate -> education, decide -> decision). 3. Suffix '-ity' (from adjectives): Denotes state or character (e.g., curious -> curiosity, pure -> purity). 4. Suffix '-ment' (from verbs): Expresses the result or process of an action (e.g., govern -> government, develop -> development). 5. Suffix '-er' / '-or' (from verbs): Forms agent nouns representing the performer of the action (e.g., teach -> teacher, act -> actor). 6. Suffix '-hood' / '-ship' (from other nouns/adjectives): Denotes state of being or relation (e.g., child -> childhood, friend -> friendship).",
     "explanation": "Evaluated on identification of morphological suffixes, correct base-part-of-speech attribution, and phonological/spelling adaptations."
    },
    {
     "id": "eng_gram1_ext_001",
     "subject": "english",
     "chapter_num": "Gram_Ch01",
     "chapter_title": "Chapter 1: Nouns — Formation & Classification",
     "type": "short_answer",
     "marks": 1,
     "difficulty": "easy",
     "question": "Why are terms of venery (collective animal nouns like 'murder of crows' and 'pride of lions') so poetically unique in the English language?",
     "options": [],
     "answer": "Collective terms for animals, known as 'terms of venery', originated in 15th-century English hunting treatises (notably 'The Boke of Saint Albans' by Dame Juliana Berners in 1486). They were created by aristocratic hunters as specialized cultural shibboleths—a test of elite education and etiquette rather than purely scientific terminology!",
     "explanation": "Highlights the linguistic history of English collective nouns."
    },
    {
     "id": "eng_gram1_ext_002",
     "subject": "english",
     "chapter_num": "Gram_Ch01",
     "chapter_title": "Chapter 1: Nouns — Formation & Classification",
     "type": "short_answer",
     "marks": 1,
     "difficulty": "easy",
     "question": "Can a noun belong to both concrete and abstract categories depending on its context?",
     "options": [],
     "answer": "Yes! Nouns like 'music', 'sculpture', and 'aroma' can be understood concretely when referring to the physical auditory waves, stone medium, or airborne chemical molecules, but abstractly when discussing the expressive artistic discipline, aesthetic experience, or emotional aura they evoke.",
     "explanation": "Illustrates contextual polysemy in lexical semantics."
    }
