    {
     "id": "eng_gram2_la_001",
     "subject": "english",
     "chapter_num": "Gram_Ch02",
     "chapter_title": "Chapter 2: Countable & Uncountable Nouns",
     "type": "long_answer",
     "marks": 5,
     "difficulty": "hard",
     "question": "Elaborate on the three historical sources of irregular plurals in the English language: Germanic vowel mutation, Old English suffixation, and classical Greco-Latin borrowings. Provide two clear examples for each class.",
     "options": [],
     "answer": "The apparent irregularity of modern English plurals reflects three distinct linguistic inheritances: 1. Germanic Vowel Mutation (I-mutation/Ablaut): In Proto-Germanic, an /i/ in the plural ending caused the root vowel to shift frontward. When the suffix eroded, only the mutated vowel survived (e.g., 'foot' -> 'feet', 'tooth' -> 'teeth', 'mouse' -> 'mice', 'goose' -> 'geese'). 2. Old English Weak Suffixation: Old English had multiple noun declensions. The weak declension formed plurals with '-an' / '-en'. While standard Middle English adopted the Northern '-s' marker, survivals include 'ox' -> 'oxen' and double-plurals like 'child' -> 'children' and 'brother' -> 'brethren'. 3. Classical Greco-Latin Borrowings: Renaissance and scientific adoption borrowed nouns intact with their original foreign inflections: • Latin -us -> -i (e.g., 'radius' -> 'radii', 'cactus' -> 'cacti') • Latin -um -> -a (e.g., 'bacterium' -> 'bacteria', 'datum' -> 'data') • Greek -is -> -es (e.g., 'crisis' -> 'crises', 'thesis' -> 'theses') • Greek -on -> -a (e.g., 'criterion' -> 'criteria', 'phenomenon' -> 'phenomena').",
     "explanation": "Evaluated on diachronic understanding of I-mutation, Old English weak plurals, and Greco-Latin inflectional morphology."
    },
    {
     "id": "eng_gram2_la_002",
     "subject": "english",
     "chapter_num": "Gram_Ch02",
     "chapter_title": "Chapter 2: Countable & Uncountable Nouns",
     "type": "long_answer",
     "marks": 5,
     "difficulty": "hard",
     "question": "Detail the linguistic tests used to distinguish Countable Nouns from Uncountable Nouns. Explain the role of Partitives and Quantifiers (much/many, few/little) in mediating countable expressions of mass nouns.",
     "options": [],
     "answer": "1. Linguistic Diagnostic Tests: • Cardinal Enumeration Test: Countable nouns admit direct numerical counting ('three chairs'); uncountable nouns reject direct numerals (*'three furnitures'). • Article Selection Test: Countable singular nouns obligatorily require determiners ('a pencil', 'the dog'); uncountable nouns occur freely without indefinite articles ('milk is nutritious'). • Pluralization Test: Countable nouns accept the plural morpheme '-s/-es'; uncountable nouns do not form standard plurals without shifting meaning. 2. Role of Quantifiers: • Countable nouns take 'many', 'a few', 'fewer', and 'number of' (e.g., 'many books', 'fewer mistakes'). • Uncountable nouns take 'much', 'a little', 'less', and 'amount of' (e.g., 'much patience', 'less sugar'). 3. Role of Partitives: To express specific discrete quantities of uncountable mass nouns, English uses 'partitive constructions' combining a countable unit noun + 'of' + uncountable noun: • A grain of sand / rice • A piece of advice / information / furniture • A bar of soap / chocolate • A drop / liter of water.",
     "explanation": "Evaluated on diagnostic grammar tests, quantifier distribution rules, and partitive syntactic construction."
    },
    {
     "id": "eng_gram2_ext_001",
     "subject": "english",
     "chapter_num": "Gram_Ch02",
     "chapter_title": "Chapter 2: Countable & Uncountable Nouns",
     "type": "short_answer",
     "marks": 1,
     "difficulty": "easy",
     "question": "Why do paired tools and garments like 'scissors', 'trousers', and 'glasses' only exist in the plural?",
     "options": [],
     "answer": "These words are known as 'pluralia tantum' (Latin for 'plural only'). Because scissors, glasses, and trousers were historically manufactured as two distinct symmetrical halves hinged or joined together, English grammatically conceptualized them as inherently dual objects, requiring 'a pair of' to count them!",
     "explanation": "Historical origin of pluralia tantum items."
    },
    {
     "id": "eng_gram2_ext_002",
     "subject": "english",
     "chapter_num": "Gram_Ch02",
     "chapter_title": "Chapter 2: Countable & Uncountable Nouns",
     "type": "short_answer",
     "marks": 1,
     "difficulty": "easy",
     "question": "Can uncountable nouns ever be used in the plural?",
     "options": [],
     "answer": "Yes, through a linguistic process called 'coercion' or 'type-shifting'! When an uncountable noun like 'wine', 'cheese', or 'coffee' is pluralized ('wines', 'cheeses', 'three coffees'), it shifts semantically to denote 'different varieties/types' of wine/cheese, or 'individual ordered servings/cups' of coffee!",
     "explanation": "Explains semantic coercion in culinary nouns."
    }
