     "options": [],
     "answer": "In Early Modern English, 'thou/thee' was the informal singular pronoun and 'ye/you' was the polite singular or plural pronoun (similar to French tu/vous). Over the 16th and 17th centuries, social politeness led speakers to address everyone with the polite 'you', eventually driving 'thou' to extinction outside historical or liturgical contexts!",
     "explanation": "Linguistic evolution of the second-person pronoun system."
