    {
     "id": "eng_gram5_la_001",
     "subject": "english",
     "chapter_num": "Gram_Ch05",
     "chapter_title": "Chapter 5: Adjectives — Classification & Comparison",
     "type": "long_answer",
     "marks": 5,
     "difficulty": "hard",
     "question": "Explain the OSASCOMP hierarchy governing the Royal Order of Adjectives in English. Define each of the eight categories and construct three original multi-adjective phrases that adhere precisely to this syntax.",
     "options": [],
     "answer": "The Royal Order of Adjectives in English follows an instinctive cognitive sequence moving from subjective evaluation to intrinsic physical essence: 1. Opinion / Observation: Subjective human assessment (e.g., exquisite, terrible, brilliant). 2. Size: Physical dimensions and scale (e.g., gigantic, tiny, tall). 3. Age: Chronological duration or era (e.g., vintage, modern, ancient). 4. Shape: Geometrical form (e.g., rectangular, spherical, oval). 5. Colour: Visual hue or chroma (e.g., crimson, turquoise, golden). 6. Origin: Geographical, national, or cultural source (e.g., Persian, Japanese, Swiss). 7. Material: Physical substance of composition (e.g., silk, titanium, wooden). 8. Purpose: Functional intent or use, frequently a gerund or noun adjunct (e.g., dining, sleeping, climbing). Illustrative Multi-Adjective Examples: • 'An exquisite (1) tiny (2) antique (3) circular (4) silver (5) Swiss (6) pocket (8) watch.' • 'A magnificent (1) large (2) ancient (3) rectangular (4) white (5) Greek (6) marble (7) temple.' • 'A comfortable (1) small (2) modern (3) blue (5) Italian (6) leather (7) driving (8) seat.'",
     "explanation": "Evaluated on systematic breakdown of all 8 OSASCOMP categories and construction of grammatically flawless multi-tiered adjective strings."
    },
    {
     "id": "eng_gram5_la_002",
     "subject": "english",
     "chapter_num": "Gram_Ch05",
     "chapter_title": "Chapter 5: Adjectives — Classification & Comparison",
     "type": "long_answer",
     "marks": 5,
     "difficulty": "hard",
     "question": "Describe the rules governing the formation of Comparative and Superlative degrees of adjectives in English, covering: (a) monosyllabic words, (b) disyllabic words ending in '-y', (c) poly-syllabic words, and (d) irregular comparison systems. Provide two examples for each class.",
     "options": [],
     "answer": "The formation of comparative and superlative degrees in English is morphophonologically determined by syllable count and historical stem evolution: 1. Monosyllabic (One-Syllable) Adjectives: • Add '-er' for comparative and '-est' for superlative (preceded by 'the'). Consonants following a single short vowel double (e.g., big -> bigger -> biggest; bright -> brighter -> brightest). 2. Disyllabic Adjectives Ending in '-y': • The terminal 'y' mutates to 'i', appending '-er' and '-est' (e.g., heavy -> heavier -> heaviest; happy -> happier -> happiest). 3. Poly-syllabic (Two or More Syllables) Adjectives: • Prefix periphrastic 'more' for comparative and 'most' for superlative without altering the base adjective (e.g., intelligent -> more intelligent -> most intelligent; remarkable -> more remarkable -> most remarkable). 4. Irregular Mutation Comparisons: • Root vowel changes or suppletive stems preserved from Old English: - good/well -> better -> best - bad/ill -> worse -> worst - far -> farther/further -> farthest/furthest - little -> less -> least - much/many -> more -> most.",
     "explanation": "Evaluated on syllabic rules, phonological orthography (-y to -i, doubling consonants), periphrastic 'more/most', and historical suppletion."
    },
    {
     "id": "eng_gram5_ext_001",
     "subject": "english",
     "chapter_num": "Gram_Ch05",
     "chapter_title": "Chapter 5: Adjectives — Classification & Comparison",
     "type": "short_answer",
     "marks": 1,
     "difficulty": "easy",
     "question": "Why do native English speakers instinctively say 'great green dragon' instead of 'green great dragon' without ever consciously studying OSASCOMP?",
     "options": [],
     "answer": "In his book 'The Elements of Eloquence', linguist Mark Forsyth demonstrated that all native English speakers follow OSASCOMP unconsciously! Saying 'a green great dragon' violates deep grammatical intuition, sounding wildly unnatural because opinion and size ('great') must precede colour ('green')!",
     "explanation": "Cognitive psychology of subconscious adjective hierarchy."
    },
    {
     "id": "eng_gram5_ext_002",
     "subject": "english",
     "chapter_num": "Gram_Ch05",
     "chapter_title": "Chapter 5: Adjectives — Classification & Comparison",
     "type": "short_answer",
     "marks": 1,
     "difficulty": "easy",
     "question": "What is the rule of 'Ablaut Reduplication' that can overrule the OSASCOMP order of adjectives?",
     "options": [],
     "answer": "Ablaut Reduplication is a linguistic sound rule dictating that when vowels repeat in word pairings, they MUST follow the order I &rarr; A &rarr; O! That is why we say 'Big Bad Wolf' (I before A) instead of 'Bad Big Wolf' (which OSASCOMP would ordinarily suggest)! Sound rhythm trumps word order!",
     "explanation": "Phonological constraint of I-A-O ablaut reduplication."
    }
