    {
     "id": "eng_gram6_la_001",
     "subject": "english",
     "chapter_num": "Gram_Ch06",
     "chapter_title": "Chapter 6: Determiners",
     "type": "long_answer",
     "marks": 5,
     "difficulty": "hard",
     "question": "Provide a comprehensive classification of the five major classes of Determiners in English: Articles, Demonstratives, Possessives, Quantifiers, and Distributives. Explain how determiners differ functionally from descriptive adjectives.",
     "options": [],
     "answer": "1. Functional Distinction from Descriptive Adjectives: • Descriptive adjectives express intrinsic attributes (colour, size, shape, quality) and can be graded ('tall', 'taller', 'tallest'). • Determiners express reference, definiteness, quantity, or possession; they cannot be graded and occupy the mandatory initial slot of a noun phrase before descriptive adjectives ('these three fine horses'). 2. Five Major Classes of Determiners: • Articles: Signal definiteness. 'A/An' (indefinite, non-specific singular countable reference based on acoustic sounds) and 'The' (definite, unique, or previously identified reference). • Demonstratives: Anchor nouns in spatial or temporal proximity: 'This/These' (proximal) vs 'That/Those' (distal). • Possessives: Attribute association/ownership directly to a following noun: my, our, your, his, her, its, their. • Quantifiers: Specify quantity without exact numbers: countable (many, few, several), uncountable (much, little), and dual-distribution (some, any, all, most). • Distributives: Individualize members of a group: each (definite group members), every (universal members), either (one of two), neither (none of two).",
     "explanation": "Evaluated on syntactic distinction from adjectives and systematic coverage of all five determiner classes."
    },
    {
     "id": "eng_gram6_la_002",
     "subject": "english",
     "chapter_num": "Gram_Ch06",
     "chapter_title": "Chapter 6: Determiners",
     "type": "long_answer",
     "marks": 5,
     "difficulty": "hard",
     "question": "Analyze the nuanced grammatical and pragmatic distinctions between: (a) 'few' vs 'a few', (b) 'little' vs 'a little', and (c) 'some' vs 'any'. Provide contrasting sentence pairs illustrating how choice of determiner alters meaning and tone.",
     "options": [],
     "answer": "The choice among these quantifiers expresses pragmatic polarity and attitude: 1. 'Few' vs 'A Few' (Countable Plural Nouns): • 'A few' has positive polarity, emphasizing the presence of a small number (e.g., 'I have a few close friends in town' = I feel supported and pleased). • 'Few' has negative polarity, emphasizing scarcity and bordering on none (e.g., 'He has few friends in town' = he feels lonely and isolated). 2. 'Little' vs 'A Little' (Uncountable Mass Nouns): • 'A little' has positive polarity, meaning 'some amount' (e.g., 'There is a little milk left in the bottle' = enough to make a cup of tea). • 'Little' has negative polarity, meaning 'almost none' (e.g., 'There is little milk left' = insufficient, we need to buy more immediately). 3. 'Some' vs 'Any': • 'Some' is standard in affirmative declarations ('I bought some fruit') and polite offers/requests where yes is expected ('Would you like some tea?'). • 'Any' is standard in negative clauses ('I did not see any dolphins') and open, neutral interrogatives ('Do you have any questions?').",
     "explanation": "Evaluated on precise explanation of positive vs negative polarity, countability constraints, and pragmatic tone shifts in contrasting sentence pairs."
    },
    {
     "id": "eng_gram6_ext_001",
     "subject": "english",
     "chapter_num": "Gram_Ch06",
     "chapter_title": "Chapter 6: Determiners",
     "type": "short_answer",
     "marks": 1,
     "difficulty": "easy",
     "question": "Why do we say 'an apron' and 'an adder' when historically the words were 'a napron' and 'a naddre'?",
     "options": [],
     "answer": "This fascinating historical phenomenon is called 'metanalysis' or 'faulty juncture'! In Middle English, speakers misdivided the boundary between the indefinite article 'an' and the noun: 'a napron' became heard and written as 'an apron', and 'a naddre' (snake) became 'an adder'!",
     "explanation": "Historical linguistics of rebracketing with the indefinite article 'an'."
    },
    {
     "id": "eng_gram6_ext_002",
     "subject": "english",
     "chapter_num": "Gram_Ch06",
     "chapter_title": "Chapter 6: Determiners",
     "type": "short_answer",
     "marks": 1,
     "difficulty": "easy",
     "question": "Why do country names like 'the Netherlands' and 'the Philippines' take 'the', while 'India' and 'France' do not?",
     "options": [],
     "answer": "Proper country names generally omit articles. However, countries with plural political names (the Netherlands, the Philippines) or descriptive political compounds containing words like 'Republic', 'Kingdom', or 'States' (the United States, the United Kingdom) obligatorily take the definite article 'the'!",
     "explanation": "Rules governing definite articles with toponyms and political entities."
    }
